   "verdict": "Part 1 scales internal energy from 0.7 V to 0.517 V by (0.517/0.7)^2 = 0.5455. SPICE measures 0.474 to 0.515 for the same cells, so the V^2 rule is CONSERVATIVE by 13-6% -- the short-circuit and internal-node terms fall faster than V^2, as the short-circuit sweep also shows. Part 1's 0.517 V column is therefore a slight overestimate, in the direction it already claims."
  },
  "what": "The second run, added after review.\n\n    Three things the first version could not say:\n      1. how much of FAx1's measured internal energy was the 0.5 fF load the\n         deck hung on its carry-out pin, which `internal = rail - C_load V^2`\n         never subtracted;\n      2. how far a full adder's arc energy moves with its hold state;\n      3. whether the schematic netlist really runs UNDER the liberty for\n         every cell, which the first version generalised from one."
